namespace hash;

/**
 * Hashing, as an **overload set** rather than an interface.
 *
 * `map<K, V>` needs a number for a key, and the way it asks for one is `hash::of($key)`. Everything in
 * this file exists to answer that call for the types a program already has, and to let you answer it for
 * a type of your own.
 *
 * ## Why an overload set and not a `contract::`
 *
 * A `contract::hashable` with a `hash()` requirement would read better, and it cannot work: a
 * *primitive* cannot declare a conformance, so `map<int32, V>` and `map<string, V>` - the two maps
 * everybody writes first - would be refused by their own key constraint. An overload set has no such
 * limit, because an overload's parameter type can be anything at all.
 *
 * It is worth noticing that this is the mirror image of the argument `array<T>` makes about its
 * elements. There, opt-in conformance was rejected because a type that *forgets* to opt in goes back to
 * being bit-copied in silence. Here it is rejected because a type that *cannot* opt in is excluded
 * outright. Same shape, opposite failure.
 *
 * ## Adding your own
 *
 * A type of your own joins the set by declaring another `of` in this same namespace:
 *
 *     namespace hash;
 *
 *     function of(const point& $p) : uint64
 *     {
 *         return combine(of($p->x), of($p->y));
 *     }
 *
 * What makes that work is that a namespace is found or created *by name*, so every `namespace hash;`
 * file in a program lands on the same namespace object and every `of` in them shares one overload set -
 * the set `map<K, V>`'s own body resolves against. Your declaration reaches the standard library's body
 * with nothing registered anywhere to make it happen.
 *
 * **It has to be a file of its own, and that is a real cost rather than a style note.** A namespace is a
 * file-level statement, so a `namespace hash;` file cannot also hold your `struct point`, its
 * `operator ==`, or the program using them. A single-file program cannot extend this set at all.
 *
 * Two ways to get it wrong, both of which look like nothing happening:
 *
 *  - **a nested namespace.** `namespace app::hash;` is a different namespace. `hash::of` will not find
 *    what is in it, and inside that file a bare `of(...)` *hides* the real set rather than extending it,
 *    because an overload set stops at the first namespace with a candidate for the name.
 *  - **the root namespace.** `hash::of` is a qualified name, and a qualified name is looked up in
 *    exactly that namespace rather than walked outward. An `of` at the root is invisible to it.
 *
 * A key type with no overload here is a located error at `map<K, V>`'s own call site, once the key type
 * is bound. That diagnostic points into `map.eco` rather than at your own code, which is the ordinary
 * shape of every generic library body and not something particular to this one.
 *
 * ## Two algorithms, chosen by shape
 *
 * A **byte run** - a string, a view, or anything reaching `bits<T>` - is FNV-1a, 64 bit. It is the right
 * shape for a variable-length sequence: one pass, one accumulator, no length to know in advance.
 *
 * An **integer** goes through `mix` instead, and the reason is latency rather than quality. FNV-1a folds one
 * byte at a time, and each step depends on the one before it - so an `int64` key is eight dependent
 * multiplies before the table can even be indexed, on the critical path of every lookup and every insert.
 * A key that is already one machine word does not need to be walked; it needs to be *stirred*, which is two
 * multiplies with no loop at all.
 *
 * An integer is therefore hashed **by value rather than by width**: `of(int32 1)` and `of(int64 1)` are the
 * same number, so they are the same hash. That is a property worth having rather than one to work around -
 * a map has one key type, so nothing inside one can tell the difference, and the alternative was an
 * artefact of walking bytes rather than anything anybody chose. Signedness still separates what it should:
 * `of(int32 -1)` and `of(uint32 4294967295)` are the same bits read as two different numbers, and they
 * differ, because the widening to `uint64` sign-extends one and not the other.
 *
 * ## What these numbers are not
 *
 * Not cryptographic, not stable across architectures, and not a serialization format: `of(string "1")`
 * differs from `of(int32 1)`, and the byte-run values differ between a big- and a little-endian target.
 * This is an in-memory bucket index and nothing else. Do not write one to a file, and do not expect the
 * integer values to match another implementation of anything - only the `bits`/string side is
 * canonical FNV-1a.
 */

/**
 * The FNV-1a 64 offset basis - **the value a byte run starts from**, and the reason this one is
 * `public` where the three multipliers below are not.
 *
 * `bytes` takes a seed rather than starting from a basis itself, so a hand-written `of` folding several
 * byte runs into one hash needs somewhere to begin: `bytes($p, $n, hash::OFFSET_BASIS)` is that call,
 * and it is what `bits` and the `string::view` overload below do. A multiplier is not that - it is
 * a step of the algorithm, and nothing outside this file can do anything with one.
 *
 * The canonical constant. Note that the compiler's own `fnv1a64` - which hashes module cache keys -
 * carries a basis one digit short of this one; the two are unrelated uses that never compare a value,
 * so this file spells the real thing rather than mirroring that.
 */
public const uint64 OFFSET_BASIS = 14695981039346656037;

/**
 * The FNV-1a prime, which is `step`'s multiplier and nothing else's. `private`.
 */
private const uint64 PRIME = 1099511628211;

/**
 * The two multipliers `mix` stirs with - SplitMix64's finalizer, in Stafford's variant 13. `private`,
 * for `PRIME`'s reason: a caller composes hashes out of `mix`, `step`, `bytes` and `combine`, never out
 * of the numbers inside them.
 *
 * Odd, which is the whole of what makes each multiply a bijection on 64 bits: an odd multiplier has an
 * inverse modulo 2^64, so no two keys are ever merged by one. Everything `mix` promises rests on that,
 * and on nothing about the particular digits.
 */
private const uint64 MIX_A = 13787848793156543929;
private const uint64 MIX_B = 10723151780598845931;

/**
 * One machine word, stirred so that every output bit depends on every input bit.
 *
 * **This is what an integer key gets instead of a byte walk.** `bits<T>` would fold an `int64` one byte
 * at a time - eight multiplies, each waiting on the one before it - to produce a number from a value that
 * was already a number. This is two multiplies and no loop.
 *
 * It is a **bijection**, so it invents no collisions: each multiplier is odd, and `x ^ (x >> k)` is its own
 * kind of reversible. Two distinct keys stay distinct, which a lossy "hash" of a machine word would not
 * guarantee and which matters more here than any distribution statistic.
 *
 * **The shifts are the point, not the multiplies.** A multiply pushes entropy *upward*: the low bit of a
 * product depends only on the low bits of its operands, so `$k * ODD` alone leaves the bottom of the word
 * almost as structured as it found it. `map<K, V>` indexes with `$h & ($cap - 1)` - the low bits and
 * nothing else - so a hash that mixes only upward would collide there while looking perfectly random in
 * any test that read the whole word. Each `>>` folds the high half back down; the final one is what the
 * table actually reads.
 */
#[inline]
public function mix(uint64 $key) : uint64
{
    uint64 $h = $key;

    $h = ($h ^ ($h >> 30)) * MIX_A;
    $h = ($h ^ ($h >> 27)) * MIX_B;

    return $h ^ ($h >> 31);
}

/**
 * One FNV-1a step: folds `$byte` into `$seed`.
 *
 * The multiply overflows constantly, and that is the algorithm rather than a bug - it is defined on
 * wrapping 64-bit arithmetic. Which is also why nothing here is usable in a `const if`: the folder
 * refuses an overflow rather than wrapping it, deliberately.
 *
 * `#[inline]`, because this is the innermost operation of every key hash and it is a *call* inside
 * `bytes`'s per-byte loop. `bits<T>` is a generic instantiation and so is emitted into the caller's
 * unit, but these two are ordinary module symbols - so without the attribute nothing inlines them
 * across that boundary short of `-O`'s whole-program merge, and the `run` path performs no IR pass at all.
 */
#[inline]
public function step(uint64 $seed, uint8 $byte) : uint64
{
    return ($seed ^ $byte) * PRIME;
}

/**
 * `$size` bytes read from `$data`, folded into `$seed`. The whole of FNV-1a.
 *
 * Takes a seed rather than starting from the basis, so a type made of several byte runs can fold them
 * all into one hash without going through `combine`.
 *
 * `#[inline]` for `step`'s reason: this is the loop every map lookup and every insert runs.
 */
#[inline]
public function bytes(ptr<const uint8> $data, usize $size, uint64 $seed) : uint64
{
    uint64 $h = $seed;
    usize $i = 0;

    while ($i < $size) {
        $h = step($h, $data:$[$i]);

        $i = $i + 1;
    }

    return $h;
}

/**
 * Folds a second hash into a first, for a type made of parts.
 *
 * The composition primitive, and the one a hand-written `of` reaches for. Not commutative on purpose:
 * `combine(of($x), of($y))` and `combine(of($y), of($x))` differ, so a pair and its reverse do not
 * collide.
 *
 * **The rotate is what buys that**, and it is the whole trick: `^` is commutative, so folding two words
 * together with it alone would hash a point `(1, 2)` exactly like `(2, 1)`. Turning one side and not the
 * other breaks the symmetry before the two ever meet.
 *
 * It used to run its operand through `bytes` as an eight-byte FNV run - eight dependent multiplies to fold
 * one word that was already a hash. `mix` is the same job in two, and the same one the integer overloads
 * take, so a composite key and a primitive one are stirred by one function rather than two.
 *
 * `#[inline]` for `step`'s reason: a hand-written `of` folds one of these per field, and without the
 * attribute each is a call across the unit boundary around four instructions.
 */
#[inline]
public function combine(uint64 $seed, uint64 $value) : uint64
{
    return mix((($seed << 5) | ($seed >> 59)) ^ $value);
}

/**
 * A `T`'s **raw bytes**, hashed. The explicit spelling of the thing `of` must never do by default.
 *
 * Byte-hashing is right for a type that *is* its bytes, and wrong for every type that holds an address.
 * A `string` byte-hashed is a heap pointer hashed, so two strings holding the same text in two buffers
 * would land in two buckets while comparing equal - which is the one way a hash table breaks that
 * nothing else catches. It is wrong for a struct with padding too, since the padding is whatever the
 * allocator left there.
 *
 * So there is deliberately **no generic `of<T>`** above this. A catch-all would answer for a type nobody
 * thought about, silently. This is that capability with the decision left where it belongs: at the call
 * site, in your own `of`, where you know what your type is made of.
 */
public function bits<T>(const T& $value) : uint64
{
    // a `ptr<const uint8>` over the value's bytes, walked by value. no borrow is formed, so nothing
    // is promised and no `unsafe` is owed - the doc above is the whole of the argument about when
    // this is the wrong thing to do, and it stays advice rather than a compiler rule
    return bytes(ptr<const uint8>($value:$), mem::size<T>(), OFFSET_BASIS);
}

/**
 * The integer overloads.
 *
 * Written out one per type rather than as one generic, and the reason is the widening below: it is what
 * separates a signed value from an unsigned one, and one generic body would have nothing to widen *from*.
 * `usize` and `isize` are their own primitives rather than spellings of `uint64` and `int64`, so they are
 * their own signatures and are not redundant. `int`, `uint` and `float` are aliases and need nothing.
 *
 * Every one takes `const T&`: a lookup must not touch a reference count, and a map's probe loop is the
 * hot path that rule exists for.
 *
 * **The widening to `uint64` is the ordinary implicit conversion**, so a narrow type reaches `mix` with the
 * value it holds and a negative one arrives sign-extended. That is why `of(int32 -1)` and `of(int64 -1)`
 * agree while `of(int32 -1)` and `of(uint32 4294967295)` do not - the same bits, read as two different
 * numbers, which is exactly the distinction a key type is asking to be kept.
 *
 * **`#[inline]` on every one of them, for `step`'s reason and one level further out.** `map<K, V>`'s methods
 * are instantiations emitted into the *consuming* unit while these are ordinary stdlib symbols, so without
 * the attribute every lookup and every insert pays an opaque cross-unit call around six instructions - and
 * an opaque call is also a memory barrier, so the probe loop reloads the capacity and the key across it.
 */
#[inline] public function of(const int8& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }
#[inline] public function of(const int16& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }
#[inline] public function of(const int32& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }
#[inline] public function of(const int64& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }
#[inline] public function of(const isize& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }

#[inline] public function of(const uint8& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }
#[inline] public function of(const uint16& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }
#[inline] public function of(const uint32& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }
#[inline] public function of(const uint64& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }
#[inline] public function of(const usize& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }

/**
 * A bool has two values, so the only thing its hash owes anybody is that they differ - but they should
 * differ *everywhere*, not just in the bottom bit, or a `map<bool, V>` puts both in adjacent slots.
 */
#[inline] public function of(const bool& $value) : uint64 { return mix($value); }

/**
 * The float overloads, which are **not** a byte hash.
 *
 * `+0.0` and `-0.0` compare equal and do not share their bytes, so byte-hashing them puts one key in
 * two buckets: `$m[0.0]` would miss an entry stored under `-0.0`. Normalized here, once, where the hash
 * is minted - the alternative is every caller remembering.
 *
 * The other half of IEEE-754 has no fix and is worth knowing: **a NaN is never equal to itself**, so a
 * NaN key can be stored and then never found, whatever its hash. That is arithmetic arriving rather than
 * a defect here, and it is the reason a float makes a poor key.
 *
 * **The bit pattern is read through a pointer and then stirred like any other word**, rather than walked a
 * byte at a time. A float is one machine word, so it belongs on `mix`'s side of this file's split for the
 * same reason an integer does - and going through `bits` would leave the bottom of the hash carrying
 * the exponent's structure, which is precisely the half `map<K, V>` indexes with.
 *
 * The reinterpret is a read of storage this function owns, one word wide, at its own alignment - so it
 * needs `mem::` nothing and asserts nothing. `$normal` is a local rather than the parameter because a
 * `const` borrow has no address to take, and because the zero above has to be written somewhere.
 */
#[inline]
public function of(const float32& $value) : uint64
{
    float32 $normal = $value;

    // fires for both zeroes, because that is what `== 0.0` means over them
    if ($normal == 0.0) {
        $normal = 0.0;
    }

    // **a value, not a second typed path to `$normal`.** the old spelling took `ptr<uint32>(&$normal)`
    // and read through it, which meant one live object readable as both a float and an integer - the
    // exact shape that needs a promise. `bit_cast` copies the bytes out instead, so there is nothing
    // to promise and no `unsafe` here at all
    return mix(mem::bit_cast<uint32, float32>($normal));
}

#[inline]
public function of(const float64& $value) : uint64
{
    float64 $normal = $value;

    if ($normal == 0.0) {
        $normal = 0.0;
    }

    // **a value, not a second typed path to `$normal`.** the old spelling took `ptr<uint64>(&$normal)`
    // and read through it, which meant one live object readable as both a float and an integer - the
    // exact shape that needs a promise. `bit_cast` copies the bytes out instead, so there is nothing
    // to promise and no `unsafe` here at all
    return mix(mem::bit_cast<uint64, float64>($normal));
}

/**
 * A string's **text**, hashed - not its bytes.
 *
 * A `string` is a window and a `str::buf` handle, so `bits<string>` would hash an address and a
 * length: two strings holding the same text in two buffers would hash differently while `==` said they
 * were equal, and a map keyed on them would lose entries. So this goes through the window, and a
 * substring of a large buffer hashes as its own text rather than as its parent's.
 *
 * Two overloads and not one. A `string` argument matches the `string` overload at borrow-const, which
 * beats the `#[implicit]` view conversion outright - a declared conversion ranks below every built-in
 * one - so neither ever surprises the other.
 */
#[inline]
public function of(const string& $value) : uint64
{
    return of($value->window);
}

#[inline]
public function of(const string::view& $value) : uint64
{
    return bytes($value->bytes, $value->size, OFFSET_BASIS);
}
